# Round XXXIX Track E (Core #9 SH-lane gap): the Rust lane accepts
# Option-preserving `??` (Option[T] ?? Option[T] → Option[T]) under owner
# Option B ratification 2026-08-09, but the SH lane's GIR lacks a direct
# enum-init instruction so its lowerer cannot yet emit the extract-and-wrap
# shape. Rather than accept-then-C-compile-fail (or silently miscompile),
# the SH TC rejects the shape with a lane-specific message pointing at
# the right-nested rewrite as the immediate workaround. Wired in the
# `self_host_driver_rejects_default_op_option_preserving_sh_lane_gap`
# harness which asserts the SH driver emits `E_DefaultOpRhsTypeMismatch`
# on this program.
#
# Rust behaviour on the SAME source: ACCEPTS. To pin the Rust accept,
# the `default_op_option_rhs_accepted.gg` graduation covers a strictly
# LARGER program (Option[int] ?? Option[int] ?? int, which chains through
# the peel-outer form to reach an unwrapped int). The chain runs on Rust
# but is REJECTED on SH at the first peel — which is the point of the
# lane-specific message.
Option[int] combine(Option[int] a, Option[int] b):
    return a ?? b

int main():
    Option[int] r = combine(None(), Some(9))
    match r:
        case Some(v):
            print(v)
        case None():
            print(-1)
    return 0
